[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe bug:[22m The Rust translation incorrectly converted byte lengths to bit lengths when calling [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m and [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m directly (not through the [38;5;10mblake512()[0m/[38;5;10mblake256()[0m wrapper functions).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mRoot cause:[22m The C BLAKE implementation has a quirky API convention:[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mblake512_update(S, data, datalen)[0m — [38;5;10mdatalen[0m is in [1mbits[22m (the function internally does [38;5;10mdatalen >> 3[0m to get bytes)[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mblake512(out, in, inlen)[0m — [38;5;10minlen[0m is in [1mbytes[22m (the wrapper multiplies by 8 before calling [38;5;10m_update[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C callers in [38;5;10mhash_blake.c[0m and [38;5;10mPQCgenKAT_sign.c[0m pass [1mbyte[22m counts directly to [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m/[38;5;10mblake256_update[0m. This is intentional — the C code treats these as bit lengths, so passing [38;5;10mSPX_N = 24[0m means "24 bits = 3 bytes" of data gets processed per call. This is how the reference C implementation works and produces the correct KAT vector.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translator saw that [38;5;10m_update[0m takes bit lengths and "helpfully" multiplied all the caller-provided byte lengths by 8, converting them to actual bit lengths. This changed the semantics — instead of processing 3 bytes per call (matching C), it processed 24 bytes, producing completely different hash outputs.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFiles changed:[22m[0m[0m
1. [38;5;10msrc/hash.rs[0m — Removed [38;5;10m* 8[0m from 6 [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m calls in [38;5;10mgen_message_random()[0m and [38;5;10mhash_message()[0m[0m[0m
2. [38;5;10msrc/main.rs[0m — Removed [38;5;10m* 8[0m from all [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m calls in the KAT transcript functions ([38;5;10mkat_tr_init[0m, [38;5;10mtr_absorb_label[0m, [38;5;10mtr_absorb_u64[0m, [38;5;10mtr_absorb_bytes[0m)[0m[0m
